\import{macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{frap}
\title{Turning a facet into a puzzle move}

\p{Once the shape symmetries are known, the next question is local: how can a
turn change only the positions incident to one chosen facet while carrying
their labels along? A facet rotation extends to a symmetry of the whole shape,
but a puzzle move applies it only at positions incident to the chosen facet.
Marchetti's construction retains both entries of each incidence pair
\citet{Definitions 5--6}{marchetti2025rubik}. We use finite regular
abstract polytopes of rank at least three; the
[extension lemma](frap-000J) itself does not require finiteness.}

\transclude{frap-000K}
\transclude{frap-000L}
\transclude{frap-000M}

\p{The inverse lemma makes the local action reversible. The tetrahedron
calculation that follows is a small witness for why the facet is part of a
move's identity: two locally valid turns can act differently on the same
incidence record and need not commute.}

\transclude{frap-000O}
\p{The tetrahedron witness motivates the generated move group: reachable
labelled configurations are products of these reversible local turns. The
ordinary cube calculation then shows how the same definition compares with
visible stickers and which data it leaves out.}
\transclude{frap-000N}
\transclude{frap-000P}

\p{The local action determines which positions participate and how their
incident labels travel. [Frames and orientation](frap-000A) separate
this transport into a destination and a local orientation.}
